types: The type mapping

Every Go type has one C++ type: int and uint are int64_t and uint64_t (all gocpp targets are
64-bit), the sized integers are their stdint types, float32 and float64 are float and
double, string is gp::string (32 bytes: owner, data, length), []T is gp::slice<T> (40
bytes), [N]T is gp::array<T, N>, map[K]V is gp::map<K, V>, *T is gp::ptr<T> and func(A) R is
gp::func<R(A)>. Results (A, B) are std::tuple<A, B>.

Different Go types can share one C++ type: int and int64 are both int64_t. Where Go can tell
them apart (any(int(1)) != any(int64(1)), type switches, %T), the type descriptor in an
interface value is chosen by the Go type, not the C++ one. The results of int8, int16, uint8
and uint16 arithmetic are cast back, because C++ promotes these types to int, and signed
overflow wraps because everything is compiled with -fwrapv.

unsafe.Sizeof reports the C++ layout, which differs from Go's: a pointer is 16 bytes, a
string 32, a slice 40.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#types
See also: gocpp explain strings, gocpp explain slices, gocpp explain interfaces
Also known as: typemap, sizes

Example (types.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func main() {
    	var small uint8 = 250
    	small += 10
    	var n int = 7
    	var m int64 = 7
    	var f float32 = 1.5
    	s := "text"
    	fmt.Println(small, any(n) == any(m), f, len(s))
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/types.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/types.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./types.gp:5: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./types.gp:6: var small uint8 = 250
        uint8_t small = 250;

    // ---- ./types.gp:7: small += 10
        small = uint8_t(small + 10);

    // ---- ./types.gp:8: var n int = 7
        int64_t n = 7;

    // ---- ./types.gp:9: var m int64 = 7
        int64_t m = 7;

    // ---- ./types.gp:10: var f float32 = 1.5
        float f = 1.5f;

    // ---- ./types.gp:11: s := "text"
        gp::string s = "text"_gs;

    // ---- ./types.gp:12: fmt.Println(small, any(n) == any(m), f, len(s))
    // gp[interfaces] col 2: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    // gp[allocation] col 21: boxing int into any allocates: gp::to_iface copies the value into a new
    //     reference-counted box; copies of the interface share it
    // gp[allocation] col 31: boxing int64 into any allocates: gp::to_iface copies the value into a new
    //     reference-counted box; copies of the interface share it
        gp::fmt::Println(small, gp::to_iface<gp::any>(n, &gp::td_int) == gp::to_iface<gp::any>(m, &gp::td_int64), f, gp::len(s));

    // ---- ./types.gp:13: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
